[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hello everyone,</p>\n<p>I would like to raise a concern regarding the current definition of <code>ConvexOn</code> in mathlib4. Specifically, I think the existing definition is somewhat restrictive and does not accommodate a broader class of functions, particularly those with values in the extended real numbers (<code>EReal</code>).</p>\n<h3><strong>Current</strong> ConvexOn <strong>Definition</strong></h3>\n<p>The ConvexOn definition in mathlib4 is as follows:<br>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/666dd63743750d3bf814c328bd6a1c86ffb52c8e/Mathlib/Analysis/Convex/Function.lean#L49-L52\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/blob/666dd63743750d3bf814c328bd6a1c86ffb52c8e/Mathlib/Analysis/Convex/Function.lean#L49-L52</a></p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"sd\">/-- Convexity of functions -/</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ConvexOn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Convex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⦃</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">⦄,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⦃</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">⦄,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⦃</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">⦄,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>This definition requires that for any two points within the <code>set s</code> and any convex combination of these points, the function value at the combination is less than or equal to the convex combination of the function values at these points.</p>\n<h3><strong>Issue with</strong> EReal<strong>-Valued Functions</strong></h3>\n<p>Consider the following example of a function defined on the real line:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">EReal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">then</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊥</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">else</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊤</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Intuitively, according to <em>Convex Analysis</em> by Ralph Tyrell Rockafellar , this function should be convex because its epigraph (the set of points lying on or above its graph) is a convex set. However, according to the current <code>ConvexOn</code> definition in mathlib4, this function is <strong>not</strong> recognized as convex. The reason is that when dealing with EReal values, expressions like<code>⊤ + ⊥</code>become<code> ⊥</code>, causing the convexity condition to fail.</p>\n<p>I wonder if  there's a good way to solve this problem.</p>",
        "id": 493495971,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1736821712
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"747491\">PrinChern</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Limitation.20of.C2.A0.60ConvexOn.60.C2.A0Definition.20in.20mathlib4/near/493495971\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Intuitively, according to <em>Convex Analysis</em> by Ralph Tyrell Rockafellar , this function should be convex because its epigraph (the set of points lying <strong>on</strong> or above its graph) is a convex set.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But if it includes the points <strong>on</strong> the graph, how would you define convexity in <code>ℝ × EReal</code>? Don't you fall into the same sort of problem where there is no useful way to make convex combinations of points?</p>",
        "id": 493499828,
        "sender_full_name": "Lua Viana Reis",
        "timestamp": 1736823864
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You're right <span aria-label=\"+1\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f44d\" role=\"img\" title=\"+1\">:+1:</span> ! So I wonder if there's a good way to solve this problem <span aria-label=\"sob\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f62d\" role=\"img\" title=\"sob\">:sob:</span> </p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"571941\">Lucas Viana Reis</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Limitation.20of.C2.A0.60ConvexOn.60.C2.A0Definition.20in.20mathlib4/near/493499828\">发言道</a>：</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"747491\">PrinChern</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Limitation.20of.C2.A0.60ConvexOn.60.C2.A0Definition.20in.20mathlib4/near/493495971\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Intuitively, according to <em>Convex Analysis</em> by Ralph Tyrell Rockafellar , this function should be convex because its epigraph (the set of points lying <strong>on</strong> or above its graph) is a convex set.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But if it includes the points <strong>on</strong> the graph, how would you define convexity in <code>ℝ × EReal</code>? Don't you fall into the same sort of problem where there is no useful way to make convex combinations of points?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>.</p>",
        "id": 493500226,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1736824110
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The Rockafellar definition of convexity seems to be a separate definition to the one we have. If it has applications, then mathlib could acquire it (without touching <code>ConvexOn</code>)</p>",
        "id": 493530833,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1736841966
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can you clarify whether the definition of <em>concavity</em> is correct according to you for <code>EReal</code>-valued functions?</p>",
        "id": 493535226,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1736843817
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Rockafellar says </p>\n<p>Let <code>f</code> be a function with its values in <code>[-∞, ∞]</code>,  and let its domain be a subset <code>S</code> of <code>ℝ^n</code> . The set</p>\n<p><code>{ (x, μ) | x ∈ S, μ ∈ ℝ ,μ ≥ f(x) }</code></p>\n<p>is called the epigraph of <code>f</code>, denoted as <code>epi f</code>. </p>\n<p>If <code>epi f</code> is a convex subset of <code>ℝ^{n + 1}</code>,  then <code>f</code> is called a convex function on  <code>S</code>.</p>\n<p>We can write in lean as follow:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">][</span><span class=\"n\">SMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">epi</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">EReal</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ConvexOn_rockafellar</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">EReal</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Convex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">epi</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 493561043,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1736853065
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>The reason is that when dealing with EReal values, expressions like<code>⊤ + ⊥</code>become <code>⊥</code>, causing the convexity condition to fail.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think the condition you want isn't convexity but rather <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Set.OrdConnected#doc\">docs#Set.OrdConnected</a>. In a linearly ordered field like ℝ both notions match, but in other linearly ordered semirings they can be distinct.</p>",
        "id": 493583288,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1736860539
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am collaborating with PrinChern on developing a more general definition of convexity for functions whose values lie in the extended real numbers, with the domain potentially being a Hilbert space or a normed space. We are exploring the possibility of using the definition of Set.OrdConnected to characterize convexity but are uncertain about how it can be applied, as the domain may not necessarily be pre-ordered.</p>",
        "id": 493593860,
        "sender_full_name": "Chenyi Li",
        "timestamp": 1736863785
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, I think Violeta is confusing convexity of <em>functions</em> with convexity of <em>sets</em></p>",
        "id": 493594401,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1736863953
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Both definitions are linked together, aren't they? For a function to be convex on a set, said set must be convex</p>",
        "id": 493594552,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1736864006
    },
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"sd\">/-- Convexity of functions -/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ConvexOn'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Convex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⦃</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">⦄,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⦃</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">⦄,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⦃</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">⦄,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⦃</span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">⦄,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⦃</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">⦄,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>We can also define as this way?<br>\nOn the one hand, this definition is the same as before, and on the other hand, it is also applicable to cases where the value range is <code>EReal</code>, since we avoid  expressions like<code>⊤ + ⊥</code>.</p>",
        "id": 493595193,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1736864214
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But your previous <code>ConvexOn_rockafellar</code> definition already avoids <code>⊤ + ⊥</code> and works with<code>f : S -&gt; EReal</code> right? The epigraph as you defined is a subset of  <code>ℝ^{n + 1}</code>.</p>",
        "id": 493598585,
        "sender_full_name": "Lua Viana Reis",
        "timestamp": 1736865198
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>perhaps I'm not sure of what is the problem that we could help you with</p>",
        "id": 493599689,
        "sender_full_name": "Lua Viana Reis",
        "timestamp": 1736865495
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"571941\">Lucas Viana Reis</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Limitation.20of.C2.A0.60ConvexOn.60.C2.A0Definition.20in.20mathlib4/near/493598585\">发言道</a>：</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>But your previous <code>ConvexOn_rockafellar</code> definition already avoids <code>⊤ + ⊥</code> and works with<code>f : S -&gt; EReal</code> right? The epigraph as you defined is a subset of  <code>ℝ^{n + 1}</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, you are right. But <code>ConvexOn_rockafellar</code> can only work when the value range is <code>EReal</code>.</p>\n<p><code>ConvexOn'</code> can also work in more general situation.This definition <code>ConvexOn'</code> is equivalent to the previous definition <code>ConvexOn</code> when <code>[LinearOrder 𝕜]</code>.</p>",
        "id": 493602149,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1736866193
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"129120\">@Alexander Bentkamp</span></p>\n<p>hi~. Sorry to bother u. We have a little confusion about the definition <code>ConvexOn</code> in mathlib4.</p>\n<p>Could u please give us some  comments if convenient?</p>",
        "id": 493698995,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1736903855
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I cant contribute much to the discussion since I have written the definition only with real valued functions in mind. Nonetheless, your <code>ConvexOn'</code> function looks good to me.</p>",
        "id": 493928824,
        "sender_full_name": "Alexander Bentkamp",
        "timestamp": 1736948349
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hello! I'm here again. Recently, I struggle this problem again. I think the final answer is something like this.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsConvexOn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Semiring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">PartialOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">SMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">AddCommMonoid</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">SMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LE</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WithBotTop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">Convex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕜</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">2</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>What do u think about it ? <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"387244\">@Yaël Dillies</span></p>",
        "id": 588521954,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1776742176
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"747491\">@Zichen Wang</span>, I have thought about this again and I believe I have a solution for you. First, the issue with your <code>Function.IsConvexOn</code> is that you assume the codomain is of a certain form, which will certainly be impractical. My solution is instead to rely on the new <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ConvexSpace#doc\">docs#ConvexSpace</a>. Full code is <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/compare/master...YaelDillies:mathlib4:convex_fun\">here</a>, but here is the relevant excerpt:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Semiring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">PartialOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsStrictOrderedRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ConvexSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">ConvexSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LinearOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">w</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">StdSimplex</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Set</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/-- A function `f : X → Y` from a convex space to an ordered convex space is convex on a set `s`</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">if its epigraph `{(x, y) : X × Y | x ∈ s ∧ f x ≤ y}` is a convex set.</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">When the scalars form a field, this is equivalent to the definition in terms of binary combinations.</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">See `IsConvexFunOn.of_convexComboPair`.</span>\n\n<span class=\"sd\">Under mild assumptions, convexity of the function `f` implies convexity of the set `s`.</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">See `IsConvexFunOn.IsConvexSet`. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsConvexFunOn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsConvexSet</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 592700926,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1777890741
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If you write a <code>ConvexSpace R M -&gt; ConvexSpace R (WIthTop M)</code> such that any convex combination including <code>\\top</code>with non-zero weight is <code>\\top</code>, then you recover your definition. Note however that on <code>WithBot (WithTop M)</code> this definition doesn't handle <code>\\bot</code> correctly. You will need to use <code>WithTop (WithBot M)</code> instead.</p>",
        "id": 592701352,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1777890891
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"387244\">Yaël Dillies</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/116395-maths/topic/Limitation.20of.C2.A0.60ConvexOn.60.C2.A0Definition.20in.20mathlib4/near/592701352\">发言道</a>：</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If you write a <code>ConvexSpace R M -&gt; ConvexSpace R (WIthTop M)</code> such that any convex combination including <code>\\top</code>with non-zero weight is <code>\\top</code>, then you recover your definition. Note however that on <code>WithBot (WithTop M)</code> this definition doesn't handle <code>\\bot</code> correctly. You will need to use <code>WithTop (WithBot M)</code> instead.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This looks very nice, thanks! My understanding is that this essentially rebuilds the def of convex combinations at a lower level, making the notion of convexity more general and more fine-grained. In turn, this allows us to model more general forms of convexity, which is very much what I have been hoping to achieve.</p>",
        "id": 592808820,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1777918385
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I can build more convex-analysis results on top of this API. Of course, I already have quite a few results on my side, but if you have any preferences about what would be most useful, I would be happy to prepare PRs based on that.</p>",
        "id": 592809289,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1777918529
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes indeed, the code I shared is part of a complete overhaul of the convexity library. Its main purpose is to allow non-field scalars (which <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110031\">@Patrick Massot</span> wanted for sphere eversion five years ago) and non-module spaces (which <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"439483\">@Andrew Yang</span> and I need to prove the subdivision lemma in singular homology). I didn't think it would solve the issue of convexity of functions with extended values until I wrote down the definition of <code>IsConvexFunOn</code> today.</p>",
        "id": 592812266,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1777919323
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>What a coincidence! I guess this is part of the charm of mathematics and Lean. The same abstraction ends up solving problems from rather different directions. I’m really happy that the extended-valued convexity issue fits into this framework too.</p>",
        "id": 592815737,
        "sender_full_name": "Zichen Wang",
        "timestamp": 1777920336
    }
]